chunks.representation:
$$I = \frac{Q}{t} \qquad Q = It \qquad t = \frac{Q}{I}$$

- units: A (current), C (charge), s (time), with $1\ \mathrm{A} = 1\ \mathrm{C/s}$;
- in a single loop, $I$ is the same at every point.

$$V = \frac{E}{Q} \qquad E = VQ$$

- $1\ \mathrm{V} = 1\ \mathrm{J/C}$;
- battery voltage = energy given per coulomb; component voltage = energy delivered per coulomb;
- around one loop, energy gained = energy delivered (conservation).

chunks.concept:
For a **steady current to flow**, there must be a **complete conducting loop** — a **closed circuit** — from one battery terminal, through the components, back to the other terminal. Break the loop **anywhere** (an open switch, a disconnected wire, a blown fuse) and the current stops **everywhere** in that loop.

A persistent misconception: 'current gets used up as it goes around, so later components are weaker.' This is **wrong**:

- **charge is conserved** — the same coulombs per second flow past every point of a single loop;
- an ammeter reads the **same current** just after the battery and just before it;
- what components consume is **energy**, delivered by the charge (shown by the voltage across each component), not the charge itself.

Think of water in a closed pipe loop with a pump and a paddle wheel: the flow rate is the same all the way round; the paddle wheel takes *energy* from the water, but the water keeps flowing.
